/* Lanternweald editorial pass 14 A — six story-specific scene additions, no story branches. */
(function(){'use strict';const data=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!data||!Array.isArray(data.series))return;const revisions={"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-33":{"number":33,"title":"The Unfinished Song","content":"Greg had written four lines and crossed out three.\n\nHe showed me the page without pretending it was finished.\n\n\"Something's missing,\" he said.\n\nThe Starwake Pier stage was empty that morning. The western boards had reopened after inspection, but the public performance area wasn't booked until the weekend. A breeze rattled the spare chairs in the storage shed. Greg had brought his repaired guitar; the scratch along the body still caught the light.\n\nMaui followed us down the pier carrying a basket of breakfast rolls.\n\n\"Great!\" he said when Greg explained the problem. \"I'll give you a verse about the sea. Storms, great deeds, an enormous hero—\"\n\n\"I was hoping for something smaller,\" Greg said.\n\n\"Small hero?\"\n\n\"No hero, actually.\"\n\nMaui looked personally wounded.\n\nGreg played the four-note phrase again, the one he'd built from the ferry bell at Tideglass. The fifth note rose and settled in a way that reminded me of somebody returning after a long absence.\n\n\"See?\" he said. \"After that it needs a breath, but whenever I write words, I fill it up.\"\n\nMaui leaned on the railing. \"The problem is you're thinking too quietly. The ocean—\"\n\n\"Can you just sit with me for a while?\" Greg interrupted.\n\nThere was nothing angry in his voice. He looked tired.\n\nMaui stared. \"You don't need me to do anything?\"\n\n\"I asked because I like having you around. You're good at looking at the water without getting bored.\"\n\n\"I'm also good at a lot of other things.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nThe silence that followed made me want to jump in and explain both men to each other. Maui was trying to contribute; Greg wanted company. They could probably solve it themselves.\n\nSo I carried the breakfast basket to the bench and passed out the rolls.\n\nGreg played again.\n\nMaui lasted almost two minutes without speaking. Then he pointed toward the far end of the pier.\n\n\"That board is loose.\"\n\n\"It passed inspection,\" I said.\n\n\"It's making a sound.\"\n\nGreg stopped playing. \"What kind of sound?\"\n\nMaui tapped the railing once, listening. \"Like the line on a boat when it starts taking weight. Not danger exactly. Just something asking to be checked.\"\n\nGreg looked at his guitar. \"Huh.\"\n\nI knew the look. He'd found a thought he wanted to keep.\n\n\"Don't make him sing about the board,\" I warned.\n\nGreg grinned. \"I might.\"\n\nMaui looked pleased. \"Finally. A subject worthy of a song.\"\n\nThe three of us sat until the wind shifted. We listened to ropes clicking against cleats and water moving below the supports. The low note from the old drainage passage did not sound; Fen had recorded an ordinary tide that morning. There was no mystery demanding we stop enjoying breakfast.\n\nAfter a while Greg asked Maui, \"What's the sound you miss most from home?\"\n\nMaui didn't answer immediately.\n\nThen he said, \"The moment just before a wave breaks on the reef. People hear the crash. I hear the water pulling itself together.\"\n\nGreg nodded and played a note without words.\n\nMaui listened.\n\n\"That one,\" he said quietly.\n\nGreg tried it again. He moved two fingers along the strings, leaving a longer gap after the fifth note.\n\nI could see Maui wanting to fill it. He opened his mouth, closed it, and looked down at his hands.\n\n\"Do you want me to do something?\" he finally asked.\n\n\"Stay,\" Greg said.\n\nMaui nodded.\n\nThe answer was almost painfully simple.\n\nBefore Steven came, Tamsin walked down the safe side of the pier with a clipboard tucked beneath her arm. She heard the same faint rattle Maui had pointed out and stopped.\n\n\"Good ear,\" she told him. \"It passed the earlier check, but this wind is different. I'll log it before the next set.\"\n\nMaui looked so pleased that Greg grinned.\n\n\"See? You helped without writing me a sea-king anthem.\"\n\n\"I still have excellent rhymes available.\"\n\n\"Keep them for later.\"\n\nTamsin tested the cleat and marked the board for a second inspection before the weekend performance. It wasn't an emergency, and she didn't need the whole stage closed. The tiny distinction mattered: noticing a problem wasn't the same as declaring everything ruined, and somebody qualified had to decide what the sound meant.\n\nGreg stopped playing until Tamsin finished. He asked whether the inspection would change his rehearsal time and offered to move to the sheltered east bench if it did. She said that would be sensible, thanked him, and went back to her work.\n\nNola came up the pier with a coil of yellow rope over one shoulder. The knot in it was so complicated I couldn't tell whether it had been tied on purpose.\n\n\"You're moving?\" she asked Greg.\n\n\"Temporarily. Tamsin's got a second look at the cleat.\"\n\n\"Good. I need the east bench for the afternoon instrument return. If you can finish before then, you're welcome to use it.\"\n\nGreg checked his watch. \"Thirty minutes?\"\n\n\"Twenty-five.\"\n\nHe made a face. \"You're a tough booking agent.\"\n\n\"I'm the one who puts the chairs away when the booking agent goes home.\"\n\nMaui reached for her rope. \"I can carry that.\"\n\n\"You can ask where it goes,\" Nola said, passing it to him.\n\nI hid my smile behind a breakfast roll. He'd been about to offer to carry every rope on the pier as though strength were the only thing the place lacked.\n\nGreg sat on the steps with his notebook balanced on his knee. The unfinished song had room for another line, but his pencil hovered over the page.\n\n\"What if the missing part isn't words?\" Maui asked.\n\nGreg looked up. \"Go on.\"\n\nMaui glanced at the loose cleat, the empty stage, and Nola dragging a chair into storage. \"You stop playing when somebody needs to say something. Maybe the song needs somewhere to stop.\"\n\nGreg stared at him.\n\n\"That is either beautiful or incredibly annoying,\" he said.\n\n\"Both,\" I offered.\n\nHe laughed, then struck a chord and let it fade without filling the silence. The ropes creaked softly, and somewhere underneath the boards a gull complained about the morning.\n\n\"Yeah,\" Greg said. \"That's closer.\"\n\nMaui was grinning so hard it almost hurt to look at him. He had helped make music without demanding that anybody sing about him. Nola called from the shed to remind us that twenty-five minutes did not expand just because the tune was improving.\n\nGreg closed his notebook and picked up the guitar. He had a deadline. The unfinished line could wait for him.\n\nWe carried the guitar case to the bench. Maui looked at the cleared stage.\n\n\"I thought the sound was only something to fix.\"\n\n\"Sometimes it's a piece of a song,\" Greg said. \"Sometimes it's a safety problem. You can listen for both.\"\n\nMaui considered that, then leaned against the railing with his breakfast roll.\n\nI wanted to say something important about the way he was learning. Instead I asked if he wanted the last piece of bread.\n\n\"Obviously.\"\n\nGreg held out his hand for it first. Maui stared at him in betrayal. They argued cheerfully over the roll until I tore it in half, which struck me as considerably better music than a speech about sharing.\n\nAround midday Steven arrived carrying a small tin of snacks. He waved to me, hugged Greg, and asked whether he was interrupting.\n\n\"Not at all,\" Greg said. \"We're doing complicated artistic work.\"\n\nSteven looked at the three of us sitting silently.\n\n\"Looks intense.\"\n\n\"Very,\" Maui agreed.\n\nSteven laughed and settled at the edge of the bench. He didn't ask to hear a private song before it was ready. Greg played a bit of the new melody because he wanted to, not because his son had to be an audience.\n\nBy late afternoon the song still had only two lines.\n\nGreg looked happier.\n\nOn the walk back to Tideglass, Maui carried the empty basket. He had a strange expression, as though he'd done a difficult task and wasn't sure whether anyone had noticed.\n\n\"Did you enjoy today?\" I asked.\n\n\"I was waiting for Greg to need me.\"\n\n\"He did.\"\n\n\"I didn't fix anything.\"\n\n\"You listened.\"\n\n\"Very heroic.\"\n\n\"Not everything is supposed to be.\"\n\nHe stopped beside the sea-glass garden. The raised beds were full of the tiny, surviving signs of the season. A white flower bent in the salt wind but did not break.\n\n\"Sometimes I worry,\" he said, \"that if I'm not doing something remarkable, you won't need me.\"\n\nI took his hand. \"I don't want you because you're necessary. I want you because I like the person who sits beside me, even when there's nothing to rescue.\"\n\nHe looked down at me.\n\n\"That person says very few intelligent things.\"\n\n\"True.\"\n\n\"Terrible cook.\"\n\n\"Frequently.\"\n\n\"Excellent at carrying blankets.\"\n\n\"Legendary.\"\n\nHe laughed and drew me close enough to kiss. The kiss was gentle, a little shy, warmed by the long ordinary day.\n\nLater Greg sent a note. He had kept the space between the notes, written one new line, and left the song unfinished.\n\nBeneath it he added: *Tell Maui thanks for sitting with me.*\n\nI showed Maui.\n\nHe read it twice.\n\nThen he put the note beside the tiny wooden fish, where he kept things that made him remember the difference between being admired and being wanted.\n\nThat night he didn't tell me a heroic story.\n\nWe listened to the sea until we were ready to go inside.","word_count":1561},"maui-45":{"number":45,"title":"The Last Big Performance","content":"The invitation was written in letters three inches high.\n\nMAUI, HERO OF THE TIDE, WILL OPEN THE REPAIR CELEBRATION.\n\nHe stared at the poster with an expression I couldn't read.\n\n\"Well,\" I said.\n\n\"Well.\"\n\nI waited for him to lift his chin and begin planning a speech about himself.\n\nInstead he asked, \"Who decided that?\"\n\nPip appeared from behind the lantern table.\n\n\"The entertainment committee. They thought people would like something exciting after all the repairs.\"\n\nMaui looked around the square.\n\nThe temporary surface channel had passed its second inspection. Riverstone's bridge was almost ready for its final load test. The western Starwake boards had reopened, and the Brasswatch generator had stopped behaving like an offended dragon. The village had survived a string of storms and repairs, but the lower service passage remained closed while engineers studied its age and the old decisions surrounding it.\n\nThere was reason to celebrate.\n\nThere was also a lot of work that didn't belong to one famous man.\n\n\"I don't want to be the opening act,\" Maui said.\n\nPip blinked.\n\n\"You don't?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nI didn't answer for him.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said.\n\nThe disappointment was real. I could see it cross Pip's face. They had hoped for a draw, something that would make the square full and the donation box heavier.\n\nMaui noticed.\n\n\"I can help with something else,\" he offered. \"If you need it.\"\n\nPip looked toward the pile of lantern hooks.\n\n\"Actually...\"\n\nWe spent the next two days preparing the celebration.\n\nGreg would play a short original set, because he had chosen to and could leave when it was over. The children from Tidewake wanted to perform their little story about Fen falling into the harbor. Fen protested until they discovered they had been given the role of the wise crab.\n\nIroh offered tea. Mara brought bread. Tamsin made sure the decorations were mounted on independent supports rather than the ancient arches. Edda checked the viewing areas so people could sit or stand without blocking the routes.\n\nMaui helped Pip sort hooks.\n\nIt was possibly the least glamorous work he'd ever done.\n\nHe was very bad at sorting.\n\n\"Those are the long ones,\" Pip told him.\n\n\"They look short.\"\n\n\"Compared with your hand, everything looks short.\"\n\nI laughed from the next table.\n\nMaui held up a hook.\n\n\"This one?\"\n\n\"Medium.\"\n\nHe sighed.\n\n\"How can there be so many kinds of hook?\"\n\n\"How can there be so many kinds of fish?\" Pip asked.\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Excellent point.\"\n\nOn the evening before the festival, the volunteers met to confirm what the celebration actually required. Pip had written a cheerful program, but the schedule had no break between the children's kite display and the lantern procession. Greg was listed for three songs despite having agreed to two.\n\n\"He'll still do three,\" somebody said. \"He likes playing.\"\n\n\"Ask him,\" I said.\n\nWe found Greg sorting cables by the stage. He was happy to play two. The third song wasn't ready, and he'd promised to be back in Beach City the next morning. Nobody was entitled to turn a friendly offer into an obligation.\n\nPip revised the program without grumbling. The children chose a shorter kite demonstration because Fen had posted an afternoon wind warning. Tamsin checked that the lantern stands could be moved without blocking Edda's turning space. Mara counted the bread orders against the bakery's actual flour supply.\n\nMaui carried chairs while we worked. When one volunteer asked whether he could still give a short heroic introduction, he stopped and looked at the children waiting to test their lantern hooks.\n\n\"I'd rather introduce the people who did the repairs,\" he said. \"If they want to speak.\"\n\nSeveral of them didn't. The council decided a name could go on the public thanks list only with permission; private volunteers could be thanked collectively. One worker asked not to be identified at all. Pip crossed the name out without asking why.\n\nLater Maui admitted that he'd imagined the crowd chanting for him.\n\n\"You can want applause,\" I said. \"You just can't collect it on someone else's behalf.\"\n\n\"I know. I wish knowing didn't make me feel ridiculous.\"\n\n\"It doesn't. You look ridiculous when you try to hide behind a basket of lantern hooks.\"\n\nHe peered over the basket. \"Then my disguise has failed.\"\n\nI laughed and kissed him on the cheek. There was no grand lesson written on the program, only accurate times, safe paths, and people who'd agreed to be there.\n\nPip brought the revised poster round after sunset, its corners curling where the ink was still wet.\n\n\"Could you look before I hang it?\" they asked Maui.\n\nHe held it at arm's length. The words were smaller than before. The children's performance sat at the top, followed by the inspection-fund supper and the lantern walk. No one's name occupied half the paper.\n\nMaui swallowed.\n\n\"Are you disappointed?\" Pip asked.\n\nHe took much too long to answer. \"A little.\"\n\nPip's mouth tightened. I thought they might apologize, though they hadn't done anything wrong.\n\n\"I used to like seeing my name,\" Maui said. \"That isn't your responsibility.\"\n\n\"It's okay to have a name on the poster,\" Pip replied. \"You were helping with the benches.\"\n\nHe looked down at the volunteer list, where his name stood between Lio and Edda. \"It seems very small.\"\n\n\"So is mine,\" Pip said. \"I painted six of them.\"\n\nThat made him laugh. Pip did not join in until he stopped. Then they asked whether he would carry the sign to the square, because the paint was wet and he had steady hands.\n\nI watched him take it carefully by the wooden frame instead of holding it over his head like a victory banner.\n\nLater, while we walked through the lantern-lit market, Maui caught my sleeve.\n\n\"I know you love other people,\" he said. \"I know I'm not supposed to turn this into a competition. But I miss being somebody everyone looks for.\"\n\n\"Do you want me to pretend I don't see you?\"\n\n\"No.\" He rubbed the back of his neck. \"I want you to notice the parts nobody else looks for.\"\n\nI thought of the way he'd carried the poster so Pip's paint wouldn't smudge. Of his quiet face when a child asked a question he couldn't answer. I told him both.\n\nHis smile arrived slowly, uncertain at the edges.\n\n\"That feels different,\" he said.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nHe leaned close, then hesitated because people were walking past with armfuls of chairs. I pulled him aside by the lantern shed and kissed him, deliberately and a little awkwardly, with the smell of wet paint still on his hands. We laughed when a hook rattled above us. He didn't ask whether anyone had seen.\n\nBy festival morning, the poster had been replaced. It listed the schedule without turning any person into the center of the world. Maui's name appeared under *volunteer setup*, alongside Pip, Lio, Edda, and a dozen others.\n\nHe looked at it for a long moment.\n\n\"Do you regret it?\" I asked.\n\n\"Some of it.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\n\"I like being seen.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I think I always will.\"\n\n\"That's not a crime.\"\n\nHe looked relieved.\n\n\"I just don't want to need it to know who I am.\"\n\nWe stood together beneath the lanterns.\n\nThen Lio shouted for help with the stage ramp, and Maui went to ask what they needed.\n\nThe celebration began late because the first tray of bread had to be rescued from an overenthusiastic oven. Nobody minded. Aang helped the children launch a few kites from the safe ridge, keeping sacred Air Nomad traditions separate from a local craft festival. Toph called the whole event \"terribly sentimental\" and then refused to leave before the last performance.\n\nGreg played a song about a ferry bell.\n\nIt was quieter than the crowd expected.\n\nPeople listened.\n\nI saw Maui standing at the edge of the square, holding a spare lantern pole in one hand. He wasn't watching the stage. He was watching a child who couldn't quite reach the hook they'd been assigned.\n\nHe knelt, held the pole steady, and asked whether they wanted help.\n\nThe child nodded.\n\nTogether they hung the lantern.\n\nNo applause.\n\nNo one even turned to look.\n\nSomething in my chest ached.\n\nLater, when the crowd began moving toward the food stalls, Maui came to find me.\n\n\"Did I miss anything?\"\n\n\"The wise crab was excellent.\"\n\n\"Naturally.\"\n\n\"Greg played beautifully.\"\n\n\"I heard.\"\n\n\"And you helped Pip.\"\n\nHe shrugged.\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n\"I saw you with the lantern.\"\n\nHis eyes widened.\n\n\"You did?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked almost shy.\n\n\"Was it all right?\"\n\n\"It was lovely.\"\n\nHe let out a breath.\n\nI took his hand and pulled him behind the sheltered garden wall, out of the flow of people. The lanterns made soft gold patterns on the stone.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\" I asked.\n\nHis smile was sudden and warm.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nI kissed him. His hands settled carefully at my waist, and for a few moments the festival noise became a distant, pleasant blur. We laughed against each other when a paper fish caught in his hair.\n\n\"Your audience has arrived,\" I whispered.\n\n\"A terrible critic.\"\n\n\"Very.\"\n\nWe rejoined the crowd after the lantern keeper checked the final hooks. The donation box held enough to fund part of the next inspection, and the public ledger recorded the amount. No one promised the service tunnel would reopen because the celebration had gone well.\n\nAt the end, Pip thanked the volunteers.\n\nMaui was named with everyone else.\n\nHe clapped for the children.\n\nWhen we walked home, he carried no trophy.\n\nOnly a small bent hook he'd rescued from the sorting pile, because he thought we could use it in the garden.\n\nWe couldn't.\n\nI let him keep it anyway.","word_count":1634}},"after-always":{"after-always-46":{"number":46,"title":"A Book That Would Not Burn","content":"The fire had reached the ledger, but it hadn't finished the job.\n\nThat was how Severus described it when Neri carried the old volume into Moonroot Archive. Its cover was black along one edge, the binding split, and half the pages were stuck together by smoke and water. It smelled unpleasantly of damp ash.\n\n\"I was hoping you could read it,\" Neri said.\n\nSeverus examined the book without touching the damaged edge.\n\n\"You were hoping I could make it tell you what you want to know.\"\n\nNeri's face tightened.\n\n\"Is there a difference?\"\n\n\"An enormous one.\"\n\nI laid clean paper on the worktable and moved the kettle away. The archive's west room was cool enough to keep glue from softening and dry enough for careful examination. At least, it was when Pip remembered to close the upper windows.\n\n\"What do we know?\" I asked.\n\nNeri looked toward the door.\n\n\"During the Closed Bells, a decision was made to seal the lower maintenance route. The public record shows the date of the order, but the name of the person who authorized it is missing.\"\n\n\"Missing?\" Severus asked.\n\n\"Scratched out.\"\n\n\"Then let us call it scratched out.\"\n\nI wrote the words down.\n\nThat mattered. A missing name might have been lost accidentally. A name removed by someone's hand meant somebody had chosen for the record to become incomplete.\n\nWe began with photographs. Danse had lent the archive a simple stand to hold the camera steady. Tamsin had built a cradle so the book would not be forced flat. We recorded the cover, the water stains, and the burned margins before opening the first page.\n\nSeverus prepared a small solution to test ink on a separate damaged scrap. He did not pour anything onto the ledger.\n\n\"You look disappointed,\" he said.\n\n\"I was hoping for a dramatic spell,\" I admitted.\n\n\"A dramatic spell would be an efficient way to destroy irreplaceable evidence.\"\n\n\"That was my second guess.\"\n\nHe looked at me over his spectacles.\n\n\"Your guesses are improving.\"\n\nWe spent an hour turning pages with a thin support beneath each one. Most were ordinary maintenance records: stone deliveries, canal cleanings, broken latches, a note about replacing bell ropes after a storm. The mundanity was almost comforting.\n\nThen we reached the page with the blank line.\n\nThe date was written in brown ink. The instruction said to close the lower route until the flood had passed. Beneath it, where a name should have been, the paper was rough and pale.\n\nNeri leaned closer.\n\n\"That is it.\"\n\nI studied the mark.\n\nIt hadn't been rubbed away completely. A few threads of ink clung near the left margin.\n\n\"Can we recover it?\" I asked.\n\n\"Possibly,\" Severus said. \"But recovery is not the same as interpretation.\"\n\nHe tested the page under angled light, then with a lens. The surface showed signs of scraping. Along one edge, a faint mark remained from the original pressure of the pen.\n\n\"It may have been a signature,\" he said.\n\n\"Whose?\" Neri asked.\n\n\"Not yet an answer.\"\n\nNeri went quiet.\n\nI felt the weight of their frustration. People had died or been prevented from leaving during the Closed Bells. The valley had told the story as a warning, but too many of the specific decisions had vanished behind phrases like necessary precautions.\n\nIt was tempting to put a name in the blank and make the uncertainty end.\n\nSeverus closed the lens case.\n\n\"We need independent records.\"\n\nNeri shook their head.\n\n\"The official copies burned.\"\n\n\"Then we look for records that were not official.\"\n\nI thought of Fen's tide charts. The old cart invoices. The private departure register that had once expected people to return, whether they wanted to or not.\n\n\"Maintenance bills,\" I said. \"A closure order would have needed materials. Locks, hinges, sealing stone. Somebody paid for them.\"\n\nSeverus turned toward me.\n\n\"Excellent.\"\n\nI tried not to look too pleased.\n\nSeverus fetched a sheet of clean paper, set it beside the damaged ledger, and wrote three headings in his narrow, severe hand: *observed*, *reported*, and *inferred*.\n\n\"That looks like a punishment,\" I said.\n\n\"It is one, for anyone determined to confuse those categories.\"\n\nHe slid the pencil toward me. The ledger's scorched binding rested on its support, held open at the page we could inspect without forcing the paper. The ink smelled faintly of iron when I leaned close. I could make out a figure in the margin and the beginning of a date.\n\n\"Observed,\" I said. \"A payment was recorded. Reported, the lower route was closed before the worst of the water.\"\n\n\"Inferred?\" he asked.\n\n\"Someone ordered it.\"\n\n\"Someone arranged payment,\" he corrected. \"Those may or may not be the same person.\"\n\nHe didn't say it kindly. I was annoyed enough to write *unknown* in the final column with a heavy hand.\n\nHis gaze dropped to the paper. \"A sensible answer.\"\n\nI looked up. \"Was that praise?\"\n\n\"Don't grow dependent upon it.\"\n\nI wanted to laugh, but he was staring at a line of faint writing near the binding. He had seen something I hadn't.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"The clerk wrote this number twice. Once before the seal was applied, once after. Either an error or two distinct transactions. We must not make them a single event.\"\n\nHe sounded like a teacher giving an exacting lesson. Then I noticed how carefully he kept his sleeves back from the ash, how he had positioned the lamp so my shadow wouldn't cover the page. His care seldom arrived wearing a friendly expression.\n\nNeri came in with an empty tray and asked whether we'd found the name.\n\n\"No,\" Severus said. \"We found a question that survived the fire.\"\n\nHe gave them the three-column sheet rather than the book itself. That was the first useful thing our investigation had produced all afternoon: an account honest enough to be wrong later.\n\nWe spent the afternoon following money.\n\nMara brought an old bakery account book that mentioned feeding an extra work crew during the flood. Tamsin found a receipt for iron fittings in a private box. Fen produced a copy of a tide measurement showing the lower route had been sealed before the highest water arrived.\n\nNone of those records named the person who gave the order.\n\nTogether, they changed the story.\n\nThe route had not been shut only because the flood had reached its worst point. It had been shut earlier, when officials were frightened of what might happen and had begun restricting movement.\n\nNeri sat down.\n\n\"I remember the fear,\" they said. \"I remember people arguing. Some wanted to keep the doors open. Others said the panic would kill us.\"\n\n\"Who was in charge?\" I asked.\n\nThey looked at me.\n\n\"I cannot yet give a reliable answer.\"\n\nI heard how carefully they had chosen the word reliable.\n\nSeverus did too.\n\n\"Then do not manufacture one,\" he said.\n\nThe room was quiet.\n\nAfter a while Neri asked whether the evidence should be displayed at the next council meeting.\n\n\"Show what we know,\" I said. \"Not a guessed signature. Include the scratched line. Include the invoices. And invite testimony from people affected by the closure, with their permission.\"\n\nSeverus nodded.\n\n\"An honest gap is more useful than a persuasive falsehood.\"\n\nWe prepared a report with clear labels: confirmed, corroborated, unverified. A warning at the top explained that the ledger's damaged condition limited the conclusions anyone could draw.\n\nThe work took longer than the committee wanted.\n\nThat felt appropriate.\n\nWhen Neri left, the archive was dark except for one lamp over the table. Severus stood beside the ledger, looking at the scraped line.\n\n\"People are fond of histories with a villain named in the first sentence,\" he said.\n\n\"Are you thinking about your own?\"\n\nHis face changed.\n\n\"Among others.\"\n\nI did not press.\n\nHe looked toward the window.\n\n\"I have been praised for choices that harmed people. Condemned for actions I did not take. Sometimes both by the same person, depending on which version of the story they preferred.\"\n\n\"Does that make the truth less important?\"\n\n\"Quite the opposite.\"\n\nI stepped beside him.\n\n\"I'm glad you told Neri to leave the gap.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Are you praising my restraint?\"\n\n\"Don't let it go to your head.\"\n\n\"Too late.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe touched my hand, the lightest pressure of his fingers against mine. Then he took off his spectacles and set them beside the ledger.\n\n\"I had been looking forward to an evening with you,\" he said.\n\n\"You have one.\"\n\n\"After this?\"\n\n\"Especially after this.\"\n\nHe leaned close. His kiss was gentle, touched with the faint warmth of tea and the sharper scent of paper dust. I put my hand against his chest and felt him relax.\n\n\"Come home,\" I said.\n\nHe glanced at the old book.\n\n\"It will be here tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe nodded, as though he were still getting used to the idea that leaving work unfinished did not mean abandoning it.\n\nWe covered the ledger, locked the archive, and walked through the Moonroot Door.\n\nIn the morning, the book remained exactly where it belonged: damaged, preserved, and still capable of telling a story without pretending it could tell all of one.","word_count":1527},"after-always-48":{"number":48,"title":"The Lantern Vigil","content":"Severus stood beneath the far arch with his coat buttoned to the throat and his hands in his pockets.\n\nI spotted him because everyone else was moving.\n\nThe Frostturn Lantern Walk had drawn people from every district, though not everyone had come for the same reason. Some carried lights painted with names. Some wore their best coats. Others had arrived straight from work, their boots still muddy from the ridge path. A child had brought a lantern shaped like an extremely unconvincing duck.\n\nGreg was playing a slow instrumental piece near the fountain.\n\nFor once, he hadn't been asked to fill every silence. The notes sat lightly in the air, leaving spaces for the sound of water beneath the old stones. People could listen, talk quietly, or ignore the music altogether.\n\nSeverus was doing none of those things. He was watching the lanterns with an expression I couldn't read.\n\nI started toward him.\n\nThen I stopped.\n\nWe had spent months learning that affection did not make another person's solitude a problem for me to solve. It would be easy to walk over and decide his silence was an invitation simply because I wanted to be beside him.\n\nI approached slowly enough that he could see me.\n\n\"Would you like company?\" I asked.\n\nHe glanced at me.\n\n\"That depends.\"\n\n\"On?\"\n\n\"Whether you intend to describe the emotional significance of every lantern.\"\n\n\"I was going to start with the duck.\"\n\nHe looked across the square.\n\n\"That creature is anatomically offensive.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Can I stand here?\"\n\nHe moved slightly to make room.\n\nIt was a small answer. It was enough.\n\nWe watched the procession begin. Neri checked the safe route, Edda directed people away from the steps still closed after the storm, and Pip made sure the temporary lantern stands did not narrow the walkway. The lower passage toward Tideglass remained under inspection. Nobody treated the festival as permission to ignore a closure.\n\nGreg's song changed key.\n\nSeverus's fingers twitched inside his pocket.\n\n\"Do you know the piece?\" I asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Do you like it?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"It doesn't insist on being admired.\"\n\nI laughed softly.\n\n\"Greg would probably consider that high praise.\"\n\n\"Then please don't tell him.\"\n\nThe air smelled of cold stone, candle smoke, and the citrus tea Iroh was serving from a stand near the bakery. A lantern moved past us carrying a painted blue wave. Another held no mark at all.\n\nI thought of the third wartime letter Severus had chosen not to open.\n\n\"Are you remembering someone?\" I asked, then immediately wished I'd phrased it differently.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Several people.\"\n\n\"You don't have to tell me who.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nWe stood quietly.\n\nAfter a while he said, \"For much of my life, remembrance was a public examination. People expected grief to resemble guilt, or loyalty, or confession. They wanted a correct answer.\"\n\n\"And here?\"\n\n\"Here there is a duck.\"\n\nI snorted.\n\nHe glanced at me.\n\n\"An important improvement.\"\n\nI covered my mouth.\n\nHe finally smiled.\n\nA small disagreement interrupted the line before it reached the corner.\n\nTwo volunteers had placed a lantern stand across the paved turning space outside the tea stall. Nobody had done it carelessly; the wind was stronger there, and the stand looked secure against the wall. But Edda stopped when she reached it.\n\n\"I can't turn around that,\" she said.\n\nOne of the volunteers looked toward the steps.\n\n\"We can carry it past you.\"\n\n\"I don't need to be carried past it,\" Edda answered. \"The stand needs to move.\"\n\nSeverus watched the conversation with his hands still in his pockets.\n\nThe volunteer flushed. \"We thought the space was wide enough.\"\n\n\"Then measure it,\" Edda said.\n\nPip fetched the chalk and a ruler, and the group moved the stand to a spot where it could be safely tethered without narrowing the route. It took less than ten minutes. The rest of the procession simply waited, talking quietly while Greg played the last notes of his piece.\n\nSeverus tilted his head.\n\n\"They corrected the design without declaring anyone incompetent.\"\n\n\"You're sounding impressed.\"\n\n\"I am assessing an interesting deviation from school staff meetings.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe looked at Edda, who had resumed her place in the line on her own terms.\n\n\"When I taught,\" he said after a moment, \"I sometimes mistook embarrassment for a useful lesson.\"\n\nI let the admission stand. I could have asked for a list of his regrets. Instead I asked whether he wanted another cup of tea.\n\n\"Later,\" he said. \"I should like to see whether this ridiculous bird-shaped lantern survives the wind.\"\n\nIt did. A child held it with both hands and grinned as though the survival was entirely their own achievement.\n\nSeverus allowed himself a small smile.\n\nI loved that he had seen the practical work and what it meant, without needing the entire evening to become a public confession.\n\nThe child with the duck lantern stopped at the edge of the path. One side of the handle had bent, and the lantern hung at a sorry angle.\n\nSeverus went very still.\n\n\"Don't touch the hot frame,\" he said.\n\nThe child froze, eyes wide. I felt my own shoulders tighten at his tone. For one awful second I saw the old classroom, the sharp correction that could turn a mistake into shame.\n\nThen he noticed the child's face.\n\n\"The handle,\" he said, more quietly. \"Not you. The metal has come loose.\"\n\nEdda came over to check whether anyone had been burned. Nobody had. She suggested taking the lantern to the repair table before anyone tried to carry it again.\n\nSeverus crouched, though his knees clearly objected.\n\n\"Would you permit me to examine it once it cools?\"\n\nThe child nodded, then looked at me. I didn't speak for them. I moved closer to the repair table so they could see where the lantern would go.\n\nGreg's music stopped across the square. Not dramatically; he had simply finished his agreed set. The absence made it easier to hear Severus explain to the child which little tab had bent and why nobody should try to straighten it while it was hot.\n\n\"You sound like a teacher,\" I said when the child had gone to find their family.\n\n\"A deplorable habit.\"\n\n\"Not always.\"\n\nHe looked at me sharply, expecting mockery. I didn't give him any. I had watched him change the sentence before it became a wound. I couldn't know what it cost him, but I had seen him do it.\n\n\"Are you pleased with yourself?\" I asked.\n\n\"Not particularly.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps I am pleased with you.\"\n\nHe looked away. Under the hem of his cloak his fingers found mine, briefly and without show.\n\n\"An intolerable distinction,\" he muttered.\n\nWe stayed until the lantern cooled. When the child returned, the repair worker—not Severus—decided whether it was safe to lift again. Severus accepted that without a single argument. The duck wobbled happily away, its lopsided light shining on the stones.\n\nThe procession moved toward Jasmine Steps. Greg stopped playing and helped carry a lantern stand to the next corner. He had volunteered for one shift, not an entire night of unpaid music. I watched him exchange a few words with Iroh, then return to the square without a guitar in his hands.\n\nSeverus followed my gaze.\n\n\"Your other companions appear to be managing.\"\n\n\"They are.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI waited for the old sharp remark. It didn't come.\n\nWe had spoken about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory often enough that he no longer treated every shared public event as a test of whether I would choose him above someone else. But understanding didn't erase the need for small negotiations.\n\n\"Do you want to walk with me?\" I asked. \"Or would you rather stay here?\"\n\nHe looked down the lit path.\n\n\"Walk. Slowly.\"\n\nWe joined the back of the procession. Nobody asked him to carry a lantern. Nobody tried to make him speak about the war. We passed the workshop lights at Brasswatch, then turned toward the safe ridge above Tideglass.\n\nMaui was helping a family settle blankets on a bench. He raised a hand in greeting but didn't call us over.\n\nSeverus noticed.\n\n\"He's learning restraint.\"\n\n\"Painfully.\"\n\n\"A remarkable achievement.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nAt the cove, I hung a small lantern with an open amber circle beside the others. It wasn't mine to explain. Severus watched me place it on the hook and step back.\n\n\"You left it blank,\" he said.\n\n\"Not blank. Unnamed.\"\n\nHe considered that distinction.\n\n\"Very well.\"\n\nHe took a folded scrap of paper from his pocket. I pretended not to notice until he held it out.\n\nIt was a single line, written in his precise handwriting.\n\nFOR THOSE I FAILED TO ASK.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"You want this on a lantern?\"\n\n\"No.\" His voice was quiet. \"I wanted you to know I had something to say. I don't want it displayed.\"\n\nI folded the paper carefully and gave it back.\n\n\"Then it's yours.\"\n\nHe closed his fingers around it.\n\nThe wind off the sea was colder than it had been in the square. I shivered. Without comment, Severus opened the side of his cloak and drew me close enough to share its shelter.\n\n\"Is this acceptable?\" he asked.\n\n\"Very.\"\n\nHe looked down at me.\n\n\"You appear to be smiling.\"\n\n\"I am.\"\n\n\"An unwise reaction to such an undignified arrangement.\"\n\n\"Extremely.\"\n\nHis hand rested against my back. He leaned close, paused, and let me close the last distance myself. The kiss was quiet enough to belong to us, not the crowd.\n\nWhen we turned toward the path, Greg began another instrumental somewhere beyond the ridge.\n\nSeverus listened for a moment.\n\n\"Tell him,\" he said reluctantly, \"that the second piece was better.\"\n\n\"Would you like to tell him yourself?\"\n\nHe looked offended.\n\n\"Don't become ambitious.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nThe night ended without a speech from Severus, a public confession, or a declaration that he'd been transformed by festival lights.\n\nHe walked home beside me, our hands brushing inside the shelter of his cloak.\n\nI didn't need the silence to mean anything more than he had chosen to share it.","word_count":1689}},"jasmine-after-rain":{"avatar-43":{"number":43,"title":"The General's Old Map","content":"I recognized the map before I understood why Iroh had gone quiet.\n\nIt had arrived from Ba Sing Se in a flat wooden case, wrapped in oilcloth. The sender was a retired quartermaster who had heard that Moonroot Archive was collecting documents about dangerous crossings and old roads. He had included a note asking whether the archive wanted the map for its historical collection.\n\nIroh opened the case in the back room of the Jasmine Dragon.\n\nThe parchment smelled of dust and cedar. Faded lines marked rivers, supply roads, and a section of city wall drawn with the careful indifference of someone who had never lived beneath it.\n\nThe title was written in Fire Nation military script.\n\nIroh did not touch the page.\n\n\"Would you like me to put it away?\" I asked.\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\nI waited.\n\nHe took off his reading glasses, polished them with a cloth, put them on again, and looked at the map.\n\n\"Do you know what this is?\"\n\n\"An old siege plan?\"\n\n\"An old route into a city that did not invite us.\"\n\nMy stomach tightened.\n\nHe spoke with the even precision I associated with his old command voice, the one he used when a teapot was burning and someone needed clear instructions. There was no anger in it. That made the words harder to hear.\n\n\"The marks indicate where my army expected resistance to weaken. Supply depots. Water routes. Places where we believed families would run if the fighting reached the streets.\"\n\nI had read enough history to know what a siege meant. I had never seen it laid out in ink by a person I loved.\n\n\"Did you draw it?\"\n\n\"Some of it.\"\n\nHe pointed to a section near the outer wall. His fingertip stopped short of the parchment.\n\n\"I ordered a survey there.\"\n\nI sat on the bench beside him, leaving room between us.\n\n\"Do you want to tell me about it?\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"I do not know whether I deserve the comfort of telling you.\"\n\n\"That's not the question.\"\n\nHe closed his eyes for a moment.\n\n\"Then yes. Some of it.\"\n\nHe told me about the cold mornings before the attack, the officers who waited for numbers, the people who tried to turn uncertainty into confidence because soldiers needed something to believe. He told me about seeing a damaged canal and thinking of it only as a tactical weakness. He did not call his younger self foolish. He called him responsible.\n\nI had never heard him say the word with so much weight.\n\n\"People lived beside that canal,\" he said. \"I knew they did. I still marked it.\"\n\nI watched his hand.\n\n\"I can't tell you that it was all right,\" I said.\n\n\"I would not ask you to.\"\n\n\"Was the survey used?\"\n\n\"Not in the way we planned.\" His jaw worked. \"Another decision changed the course of the siege. But the fact that a plan was not carried out does not make the intention harmless.\"\n\nOutside the tea shop, someone laughed. A customer called for more hot water. The world continued in the particular, ordinary way it always did when someone was remembering what it cost to live through history.\n\nIroh turned to the note from the quartermaster.\n\n\"The archive might display it.\"\n\n\"Maybe. With context.\"\n\n\"Would you object?\"\n\n\"I'd want to know who gets to tell the story beside it.\"\n\nHe looked toward the window.\n\n\"The people who endured the siege.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe nodded slowly.\n\nWe took the map to Moonroot after closing.\n\nSeverus was at the worktable with a small magnifying glass and a stack of damaged ledgers. He glanced at the wooden case.\n\n\"Another document requiring repair?\"\n\n\"Perhaps,\" Iroh said. \"And a warning.\"\n\nSeverus opened the case and read the title.\n\nHis face did not soften. He knew enough about wars to understand that a plan could be both technically detailed and morally abhorrent.\n\n\"An archive may preserve a document without endorsing its contents,\" he said.\n\n\"Will you preserve mine?\" Iroh asked.\n\n\"If we can explain what it is and why it matters.\"\n\nSeverus turned the map toward the light. His finger hovered above a narrow road marked in red. Iroh stopped him before he touched it.\n\n\"That is not a boundary,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"What is it, then?\"\n\n\"A supply route. On that map it looks like a harmless line. I knew what it carried.\"\n\nHe did not explain further. I could hear the street outside the archive and the creak of the wooden case where it had been set on the table. The silence was different from the comfortable one I knew at the Jasmine Dragon.\n\nSeverus examined the route. \"Does the document state who maintained it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then that absence must be noted.\"\n\nIroh gave a short, humorless laugh. \"I could tell you what I remember.\"\n\n\"Memory is evidence,\" Severus said. \"It is not a replacement for the document.\"\n\nFor an instant they looked ready to dislike one another. Iroh's jaw had tightened, and Severus had narrowed his eyes in the way he did when someone mistook precision for cruelty.\n\n\"I don't want the people beneath that red line to become a footnote,\" I said.\n\nIroh looked at me. \"Neither do I.\"\n\n\"Then maybe the catalog needs to say whose viewpoint this map records.\"\n\nSeverus took a fresh slip and wrote *military planning document; civilian consequences not represented*. The words were dry. That was their strength. They didn't let the map tell the only story.\n\nIroh asked whether the quartermaster could be contacted for the provenance of the case. He did not ask the archive to publish any private address. Severus raised an eyebrow, then nodded.\n\nOutside, a delivery cart rattled past. Iroh watched it through the window with a look that made my chest hurt. I knew better than to tell him that preserving the map would heal anything.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he said to Severus.\n\n\"Don't thank me yet,\" Severus replied. \"We haven't verified the ink.\"\n\nIroh smiled, tired and genuine. \"A man after my own heart. Suspicious of anything served before its proper time.\"\n\n\"Please do not compare this to tea.\"\n\nI bit the inside of my cheek to keep from laughing.\n\nNeri joined us with the arrival ledger. They suggested a public exhibit about old routes and the Closed Bells, combining documents from multiple worlds.\n\nIroh shook his head.\n\n\"No. Not yet. The siege is not the Lanternweald's history to turn into a convenient parable. The people whose homes appear on that paper must be able to determine how it is presented.\"\n\nSeverus looked up.\n\n\"For once, I agree entirely.\"\n\nI tried to hide my smile.\n\nNeri accepted the correction.\n\nWe made a temporary catalog entry with restricted public display and a note requesting historical testimony from Ba Sing Se scholars. Iroh wrote the accession description himself, choosing words that did not conceal his role.\n\nWhen it was finished, he stared at the page.\n\n\"That is a better account than I was prepared to give.\"\n\n\"It isn't complete,\" I said.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then it can be revised when the people who lived through it speak.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nWe returned to Jasmine Steps after dark. The last lanterns glowed along the terrace. A little water had collected in a crack beside the stairs. Iroh stepped around it without thinking, then stopped.\n\n\"Look,\" he said.\n\nI followed his gaze.\n\nThe rainwater had found the shallowest path down the stone, carrying a few stray petals from the garden. It was nothing like the river on the old map, but the shape was familiar.\n\n\"I saw a water route and thought of a weakness,\" he said. \"You see it and think of the people who need to walk safely.\"\n\n\"I also think somebody should repair the step.\"\n\nHe smiled, tired but real.\n\n\"You have an inconvenient gift for returning us to work.\"\n\n\"I'm not here to redeem the past with a staircase.\"\n\n\"No.\" He took my hand. \"Nor should you be.\"\n\nWe sat on the balcony outside the tea room. The air smelled of jasmine and stone warmed by the day. Iroh brought a blanket, then asked before settling it around my shoulders.\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n\"I don't need you to tell me I'm good,\" he said after a while.\n\n\"Then I won't.\"\n\n\"But I am grateful you can love me without pretending there is nothing to answer for.\"\n\nI pressed my cheek against his sleeve. There was a trace of tea on the fabric, and the soft scratch of its weave against my skin.\n\n\"I love the person sitting here,\" I said. \"And I expect him to tell the truth.\"\n\nHe held me closer.\n\n\"That sounds difficult.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps tomorrow I will make us a particularly strong tea.\"\n\n\"With mountain mint?\"\n\n\"If you insist.\"\n\nI turned toward him. He watched my face, then met me halfway for a slow kiss that left both of us quiet.\n\nLater we took the blanket inside and closed the balcony doors against the rain. There was no grand promise, no claim that a confession had erased a war. Only warmth, the relief of being able to speak, and the work that would remain in the morning.\n\nAt breakfast, Iroh set the wooden case by the door, ready to return it to the archive.\n\nThe map was still old. The ink was still there.\n\nNow the catalog would have room for more than the general's account.","word_count":1570},"avatar-48":{"number":48,"title":"The Dragon and the Demigod","content":"Maui had a plan involving a wall of fire.\n\nI knew because he spread his arms across the Jasmine Dragon's counter and described it in tremendous detail while Iroh continued pouring tea with the expression of someone who had already heard three worse plans before breakfast.\n\n\"Imagine the lanterns,\" Maui said. \"All lighting at once. A giant flame across the terraces, then the whole sky—\"\n\n\"No,\" I said.\n\n\"I haven't finished.\"\n\n\"That's why I'm saying it now.\"\n\nIroh set down the kettle.\n\n\"Perhaps we should begin with what the festival requires.\"\n\n\"Joy,\" Maui declared.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Wonder.\"\n\n\"Certainly.\"\n\n\"A little fire.\"\n\n\"Some,\" Iroh said, \"would be permissible in a properly contained lamp.\"\n\nMaui looked betrayed.\n\nWe were preparing the closing evening of the Longlight season, when residents hung the last of the wind-marker kites and lit low-smoke lanterns along the safe route. The storm had damaged several garden steps, and Tamsin had posted a clear fire and load plan. Open flame was permitted only inside protected lantern housings away from dry cloth and the old timber supports.\n\nMaui had apparently seen the word permitted and taken it as an artistic challenge.\n\n\"People like spectacle,\" he said.\n\n\"People also like having roofs,\" I answered.\n\nIroh smiled.\n\n\"Both are worth considering.\"\n\nWe took the plan to Tamsin, who was checking a repaired gutter outside the teahouse. She listened for exactly forty seconds before taking the paper and circling three places where flame would be too close to cloth, one where the walkway was too narrow, and another where heat could damage a wooden railing.\n\n\"That,\" she said, handing it back, \"is a list of things not to do.\"\n\nMaui frowned.\n\n\"You're all very difficult.\"\n\n\"We are trying to remain alive,\" I said.\n\nIroh looked at the drawings.\n\n\"What is it you want people to feel when the lanterns are lit?\" he asked.\n\nMaui hesitated.\n\n\"I want them to look up. To see that the village is still beautiful after all this repairing. I want the children to remember it.\"\n\nIroh's expression softened.\n\n\"Then let us make something memorable that doesn't ask anyone to stand beneath falling fire.\"\n\nWe moved the planning meeting to the garden.\n\nIroh brought a box of shallow metal trays, polished on the inside. He set one beside a lantern and angled it toward the wall. The small flame reflected across the curved surface, making a broad patch of warm light.\n\nMaui leaned closer.\n\n\"That's clever.\"\n\n\"It is a simple trick of light,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"Still clever.\"\n\nI took another tray and tried the angle. My first attempt sent the reflection directly into Iroh's eyes.\n\nHe blinked.\n\n\"Perhaps a little lower.\"\n\n\"Sorry.\"\n\nMaui laughed.\n\nWe spent the afternoon testing the arrangement. With six lanterns and six reflectors, we could illuminate the old stone wall without adding more heat than the fire plan allowed. The metal would cool before being packed away. The accessible route remained clear.\n\nPip suggested small carved shapes in front of the reflectors, so the light made moving shadows on the wall when the breeze reached the lantern housings.\n\nMaui drew a fish.\n\nI drew a star.\n\nIroh drew a very round teapot.\n\n\"That is a dangerous creature,\" Maui said solemnly.\n\n\"It has defeated many impatient men,\" Iroh replied.\n\nI laughed.\n\nAt dusk, the volunteers tested the display. The light spread across the stone in overlapping gold shapes. A fish swam slowly through the shadow of a star. The teapot appeared to steam, though the effect was only light moving through a little paper cutout.\n\nChildren gathered beneath the shelter and pointed.\n\nMaui watched their faces.\n\nHe looked almost as pleased as he would have if the sky had caught fire.\n\n\"Better than the giant flame?\" I whispered.\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Different.\"\n\n\"Safer.\"\n\n\"Very different.\"\n\nIroh stepped beside him.\n\n\"One does not have to become smaller to give someone else room to see.\"\n\nMaui looked at him.\n\n\"Was that wisdom?\"\n\n\"Only an observation.\"\n\n\"You're suspiciously good at those.\"\n\nIroh smiled.\n\nThe argument moved outside while Pip checked the screens. Maui leaned against the courtyard wall and looked at Iroh as though the older man had committed a personal insult.\n\n\"You're really going to light the whole thing with ordinary lamps?\"\n\n\"They are lamps with an excellent history of lighting things,\" Iroh replied.\n\n\"Where's the spectacle?\"\n\n\"In the shadow someone wishes to show.\"\n\nMaui made a dissatisfied noise. I was about to intervene when Iroh held up a hand—not to silence either of us, but to ask for a moment.\n\n\"Tell me something, Maui. When you learned to pull an island from the sea, did you know who would live there?\"\n\nMaui stared at him. His tattoos shifted over his arm. \"Not everyone.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps a lantern belongs to whoever uses its light, not to whoever can make the brightest flame.\"\n\nI winced. It was sharper than Iroh usually sounded. Maui heard it too.\n\n\"That's a pretty way to tell me to shut up.\"\n\nIroh closed his eyes for a moment. \"It was a pretty way to avoid apologizing for having been rude.\"\n\nI looked between them. That admission mattered more than the proverb.\n\nMaui rubbed the back of his neck. \"I wasn't listening to Pip. I heard a festival and imagined a crowd chanting my name.\"\n\n\"An understandable temptation,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"Oh, you want applause too?\"\n\n\"Certainly. When the jasmine cakes rise properly, I am insufferable.\"\n\nMaui laughed so loudly that someone inside asked whether the screen frame had fallen.\n\nI took a step toward him, then stopped. I loved both men. I'd told each of them plainly that I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred polyamory; I didn't want that to make me an interpreter for every conflict they chose to have.\n\n\"Work it out between you,\" I said. \"Pip still needs the lamps.\"\n\nMaui held out his hand to Iroh. \"I'll carry them.\"\n\nIroh took it. \"I'll ask where they should go.\"\n\nThey went inside without asking me to decide who had been wiser. I stood in the courtyard for another breath, smelling hot paper and tea, and felt the strange relief of not being responsible for making two people I loved agree.\n\nBefore the public was invited inside, Pip asked us to test the screens once more. A paper edge had curled near a candle. Nobody had been hurt, but the demonstration wasn't going to open while somebody could brush a hand against hot paper.\n\nMaui opened his mouth. I could almost see his argument assembling itself: he could move fire safely, he had faced the sea, he had personally solved problems much larger than this.\n\nIroh put a hand on his arm. \"Let them finish.\"\n\nMaui looked betrayed. Then he glanced at the child standing beside the frame, clutching a paper boat they'd made.\n\n\"Will it be all right?\" the child asked.\n\n\"After we move the lamp,\" Pip answered. \"If you'd like, you can help choose where your boat's shadow goes.\"\n\n\"Only for one song.\"\n\n\"Then only for one song.\"\n\nWe adjusted the frame and waited for it to cool. Maui held the little boat as carefully as if it were made of glass. He asked the child whether the shadow looked right. The child shook their head, so he moved it to the left.\n\n\"Better?\"\n\n\"Perfect.\"\n\nI laughed at the pride on his face. Iroh leaned close enough to speak quietly.\n\n\"You chose well when you told him the sky did not need catching fire,\" he said.\n\n\"I chose self-preservation. We'd still be arguing about insurance.\"\n\nHe chuckled.\n\nA vendor came to ask whether we wanted more lantern oil. Pip checked the inventory before agreeing to buy enough for the scheduled display. Nobody was being asked to donate supplies they hadn't promised. The little festival felt less magical for all its lists and receipts, but it was also less likely to leave someone cleaning up alone at midnight.\n\nIroh touched my hand. \"Will you stay beside me for the opening?\"\n\n\"Yes. Afterward I'm helping Maui and the children put away the reflectors.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You don't mind?\"\n\n\"I may wish I had more time. That is different from claiming yours.\"\n\nI smiled at him. We'd spoken openly about my being ethically non-monogamous and preferring polyamory. Our relationships were chosen, not ranked, and neither man had to pretend to feel nothing about sharing time.\n\n\"Then I'll find you when the last lantern goes out,\" I said.\n\n\"With tea?\"\n\n\"With tea.\"\n\nHe glanced at my mouth, then asked if he might kiss me before the crowd arrived. I stepped closer. His kiss was slow and warm, with the faint taste of jasmine that always seemed to linger on his lips.\n\nWhen we parted, Maui was balancing the child's boat on one finger.\n\n\"Don't,\" I called.\n\n\"I wasn't!\"\n\nThe obvious lie made us all laugh, including the child. The little boat survived the evening, and so did the paper screens.\n\nThe display opened that evening. The first visitor asked how the shadows worked. Pip explained the reflectors. Another asked whether the light was magic. Iroh showed them the metal tray and let them examine it after it cooled.\n\nMaui stayed at the side of the path, helping children place their own paper shapes at a safe distance from the lanterns.\n\nNo one needed to be dazzled into gratitude.\n\nAfter the last lantern was extinguished, Iroh and I cleaned the trays. His sleeves were rolled to the elbow, and there was a little soot on his wrist.\n\n\"You're quiet,\" I said.\n\n\"I am enjoying the silence after so many questions.\"\n\n\"Did you mind working with Maui?\"\n\n\"Not at all.\"\n\n\"Really?\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\n\"He reminds me of people who once believed greatness required everyone to be looking at them.\"\n\nI tilted my head.\n\n\"People you knew?\"\n\n\"One or two.\"\n\n\"One of them you?\"\n\nHis smile turned rueful.\n\n\"Possibly.\"\n\nI dried the last tray.\n\n\"I love him,\" I said. \"And I love you. I'm glad you can share a project without having to decide what that means for your relationship with each other.\"\n\nIroh took my hand.\n\n\"We are not required to be the same kind of companion to care for the same person.\"\n\nThe words warmed me more than the lanterns had.\n\nHe kissed my fingers, then waited while I moved closer.\n\n\"May I?\" he asked.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nHis kiss was gentle and a little amused, as though he'd found another secret worth keeping. The kitchen smelled of tea and soot, and the last lamp glowed beside the door.\n\nThe next morning, Maui delivered the reflectors back to storage with a handwritten note.\n\nTHANK YOU FOR LETTING THE FISH BE HEROIC.\n\nIroh pinned it above the worktable.\n\nFor weeks afterward, it made him smile.","word_count":1784}}};for(const series of data.series){const byId=revisions[series.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of series.chapters){const update=byId[ch.id];if(!update||ch.chapter_number!==update.number)continue;ch.content=update.content;ch.word_count=update.word_count;ch.title=update.title;}}})();
